---
import LegalLayout from "../layouts/LegalLayout.astro";
import { ENTITY } from "../data/legal";

const UPDATED = "2026-10-12";
---

<LegalLayout
	title="Clinical Governance & Safeguarding"
	description="How MedicSupport Network regulates its clinicians, verifies credentials, and handles safeguarding and complaints."
	updated={UPDATED}
>
	<h2>Who this applies to</h2>
	<p>
		This describes how {ENTITY.tradingName} operates the clinical side of the group — our own
		clinicians, and the hospitals and clinics we run. It applies to every country in which we
		operate, alongside — never instead of — the professional and healthcare regulation of that
		country.
	</p>

	<h2>Our clinicians are our own employees</h2>
	<p>
		Every clinician working under the {ENTITY.tradingName} name is employed by the group, on
		the group's payroll, and subject to the group's own clinical governance, appraisal,
		safeguarding and disciplinary procedures. This includes clinicians seconded to hospitals
		in Europe and America: they remain our employees throughout, and remain accountable to us,
		even where a partner hospital holds day-to-day supervision.
	</p>

	<h2>Registration and scope of practice</h2>
	<p>
		Before any clinical role begins, we confirm current registration with the relevant
		professional body and confirm that the registration covers the work being assigned.
		Registration is re-checked at defined intervals and on any change of employer, country or
		specialism. A clinician is not placed in a role their registration does not cover.
	</p>
	<p>Our regulatory registrations are: {ENTITY.regulators}.</p>

	<h2>Credential verification</h2>
	<p>
		Where we publish a clinician profile or issue a credential reference, the record states
		<strong>what was verified, against which register, and on what date</strong>. We publish
		records we have verified and nothing else.
	</p>
	<p>
		A verification confirms identity and registration status on the stated date. It is not a
		fitness-to-practise certificate, not a statement of current clinical competence, and not an
		endorsement of a specific treatment. Full terms are in our
		<a href="/terms">Terms of Use</a>.
	</p>

	<h2>Safeguarding</h2>
	<p>
		We operate facilities where vulnerable people — including children, older adults and
		patients with reduced capacity — are treated. Every member of clinical staff is required
		to complete safeguarding training and to report concerns. We have a named safeguarding
		lead at each facility and a central escalation route to group level.
	</p>
	<p>
		Concerns about the safety of a child or a vulnerable adult are raised with the safeguarding
		lead and escalated immediately to the statutory authority in the country concerned. We
		cooperate with any statutory investigation.
	</p>

	<h2>Clinical incidents and complaints</h2>
	<p>
		Clinical incidents, near misses and adverse events are recorded and reviewed. Serious
		incidents are escalated to group clinical governance and, where the law requires, to the
		statutory regulator.
	</p>
	<p>
		Complaints about care received at one of our facilities are handled by that facility
		under the complaints procedure of the country it operates in, and — where the patient
		was treated elsewhere — we will still investigate a complaint about the conduct of one of
		our clinicians.
	</p>

	<h2>Raising a concern about a clinician</h2>
	<p>
		If you wish to raise a concern about a clinician carrying our credential, contact us at
		<a href={`mailto:${ENTITY.generalEmail}`}>{ENTITY.generalEmail}</a> with the reference
		number shown on the credential. We will acknowledge it, investigate it, and tell you the
		outcome in line with the applicable procedure.
	</p>
	<p>
		<strong>
			If a clinician's conduct may put a patient at immediate risk, contact the emergency
			services or the relevant regulator first — do not wait for our reply.
		</strong>
	</p>

	<h2>Clinical audit and quality</h2>
	<p>
		Facilities are subject to internal clinical audit covering patient safety, infection
		control, medicines management, record keeping and consent. Findings are reported to group
		clinical governance and actions are tracked to completion.
	</p>

	<h2>Limits of this document</h2>
	<p>
		This is a summary of our arrangements, not a complete governance framework, and it does
		not replace the regulatory requirements of any country in which we operate. Where this
		page and a national regulator's requirements differ, the regulator's requirements apply.
	</p>
	<p>
		<strong>
			This document has not been reviewed by a lawyer or a clinical governance
			adviser and must be before commercial launch.
		</strong>
	</p>
</LegalLayout>